\title{Finite Transition Windows from Blockwise Integer Drift}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 28, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a finite theory for locating the first sign change of an integer-valued order parameter observed at regularly spaced endpoints. Exact-size counts define a shell imbalance, and summing shell imbalances defines a cumulative imbalance. The increment of the cumulative imbalance is exactly the newly added shell imbalance. Consequently, strict cumulative descent is equivalent to a negative imbalance in every incoming shell, while a uniform shell deficit yields linear cumulative decay. Replacing pointwise descent by strict descent only at block endpoints, we prove that a nonpositive final sample guarantees a unique first nonpositive sample, permanent negativity at all later samples, and localization between the last positive and first nonpositive endpoints. Integrality forces at least one unit of decay per sampled block and gives an explicit threshold-location bound in terms of the initial imbalance. The framework applies to finite counting processes generally; its use for formulas requires an explicitly chosen syntax, length, calculus, and bounded search procedure.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Finite counting problems often produce a signed order parameter. At each scale, one counts objects of two types and subtracts one count from the other. Positive values indicate dominance of the first type, negative values dominance of the second, and zero parity. A transition occurs when the difference first becomes nonpositive.

A motivating example arises from finite collections of mathematical formulas. After fixing a finite alphabet, grammar, length function, deductive system, and bounded proof-search procedure, one may count formulas classified as provable and formulas left unresolved. These counts may be organized by exact length or cumulatively through a cutoff. The question here is conditional: assuming negative drift, what follows about the existence, uniqueness, permanence, and location of a crossing?

Pointwise descent at every cutoff may be too strong. Data may be reported only after fixed batches, while intermediate fluctuations are unavailable. We therefore study an arbitrary integer-valued function only at endpoints $0,b,2b,\ldots,Kb$. Strict decrease at these points gives a complete sampled transition theorem. Finiteness ensures a least crossing index; integrality turns strict descent into a decrease of at least one per block.

These results do not assert that a particular foundational system satisfies the hypotheses. Incompleteness by itself does not imply decreasing density, a sharp threshold, or an encoding-independent length law. Rather, the results identify the exact conclusions justified once finite drift assumptions have been established.

\section{Shell and cumulative statistics}
Let $p,u:\mathbb N\to\mathbb N$ be finite-count sequences. In the motivating interpretation, $p_n$ counts objects classified as provable in the exact shell of size $n$, and $u_n$ counts unresolved objects.

\begin{definition}[Shell imbalance]
The signed excess in shell $n$ is
\[
s_n=p_n-u_n\in\mathbb Z.
\]
Positive, negative, and zero values denote a first-class majority, a second-class majority, and a tie, respectively.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Cumulative imbalance]
The cumulative signed excess through cutoff $n$ is
\[
C_n=\sum_{i=0}^{n}s_i.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[One-shell update]
For every $n\in\mathbb N$,
\[
C_{n+1}=C_n+s_{n+1}.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Split the finite sum through $n+1$ into the sum through $n$ and its final summand.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Shell criterion for strict cumulative descent]
Fix $N\in\mathbb N$. The inequalities $C_{n+1}<C_n$ hold for every $n<N$ if and only if $s_{n+1}<0$ for every $n<N$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The update identity gives
\[
C_{n+1}<C_n\iff C_n+s_{n+1}<C_n\iff s_{n+1}<0.
\]
Apply this equivalence at each index below $N$.
\end{proof}

Thus strict cumulative decline is exactly an unresolved majority in every newly introduced shell, not merely a consequence suggested by such a majority.

\begin{theorem}[Uniform-deficit cumulative decay]
Let $d\in\mathbb Z$. If $s_{n+1}\le -d$ for every $n<N$, then
\[
C_N\le C_0-Nd.
\]
For the deficit interpretation one ordinarily takes $d\ge0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Iteration of the update identity yields
\[
C_N=C_0+\sum_{i=1}^{N}s_i.
\]
There are $N$ summands and each is at most $-d$, proving the bound. Equality is attained when every incoming shell imbalance equals $-d$.
\end{proof}

\section{Sampled thresholds and block drift}
Let $f:\mathbb N\to\mathbb Z$, choose a block width $b\in\mathbb N$, and sample through block $K$. The observed sequence is
\[
f(0),f(b),f(2b),\ldots,f(Kb).
\]
For a genuine width statement one takes $b>0$.

\begin{definition}[First sampled threshold]
An index $k$ is a first sampled threshold at block width $b$ if
\[
f(kb)\le0
\quad\text{and}\quad
f(jb)>0\ \text{for every }j<k.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Unique block-drift transition window]
Assume
\[
f((k+1)b)<f(kb)\qquad\text{for every }k<K
\]
and $f(Kb)\le0$. There exists a unique $k_\ast\le K$ such that:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item $f(k_\ast b)\le0$ and $f(jb)>0$ for every $j<k_\ast$;
\item $f(jb)<0$ whenever $k_\ast<j\le K$;
\item if $f(0)>0$, then $k_\ast>0$, $f((k_\ast-1)b)>0$, and $f(k_\ast b)\le0$.
\end{enumerate}
Consequently, if $b>0$ and $f(0)>0$, the sampled transition is localized to the endpoint interval $[(k_\ast-1)b,k_\ast b]$, of width $b$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Consider
\[
S=\{k\in\mathbb N:k\le K\text{ and }f(kb)\le0\}.
\]
The final-value hypothesis makes $S$ nonempty. Let $k_\ast$ be its least member. Its membership gives $f(k_\ast b)\le0$, while minimality gives $f(jb)>0$ for every $j<k_\ast$.

Repeated transitivity of strict endpoint descent shows that $f(mb)<f(nb)$ whenever $n<m\le K$. Hence, if $k_\ast<j\le K$,
\[
f(jb)<f(k_\ast b)\le0,
\]
so every later sample is strictly negative. If $f(0)>0$, then $k_\ast$ cannot be zero, and minimality gives positivity at the preceding endpoint.

For uniqueness, any other first threshold $y$ cannot lie below $k_\ast$, because samples below $k_\ast$ are positive; nor can it lie above $k_\ast$, because samples below $y$ would then include the nonpositive value at $k_\ast$. Thus $y=k_\ast$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
No condition is imposed between block endpoints. The theorem certifies the first sampled crossing and a one-block endpoint window; it does not identify the first nonpositive unsampled index. Arbitrary intermediate excursions are compatible with the hypotheses.
\end{remark}

\section{Quantitative decay and threshold location}
Strict decrease between integers has a universal rate: if $x<y$ in $\mathbb Z$, then $x\le y-1$.

\begin{theorem}[Sampled linear decay]
If $f((k+1)b)<f(kb)$ for every $k<K$, then
\[
f(Kb)\le f(0)-K.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each sampled step satisfies $f((k+1)b)\le f(kb)-1$. Induction over the $K$ steps gives a total decrease of at least $K$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Threshold by initial imbalance]
Assume strict sampled descent through block $K$ and $f(0)\le K$. Then a first sampled threshold $k_\ast\le K$ exists.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The sampled decay theorem gives
\[
f(Kb)\le f(0)-K\le0.
\]
The nonpositive sampled indices therefore form a nonempty finite set, whose least member is the required first threshold.
\end{proof}

When $f(0)=a>0$, at most $a$ strictly decreasing integer steps are needed. This is sharp: $f(kb)=a-k$ first reaches zero at block $a$. If one additionally knows a decrement of at least $d>0$ per block, iteration improves the estimate to $f(Kb)\le f(0)-Kd$.

\section{Algorithms}
\subsection{Linear scan}
Given values $v_k=f(kb)$ for $0\le k\le K$, scan from left to right and return the first $k$ with $v_k\le0$. During the same pass, check $v_{k+1}<v_k$. The method takes $O(K)$ time and $O(1)$ auxiliary space when streamed. A certificate records strict descent, positivity before the returned index, and nonpositivity at it. The transition theorem then supplies uniqueness and permanence.

\subsection{Binary search}
Once monotonicity has been certified and random access is available, the predicate $P(k):f(kb)\le0$ is monotone. If $P(K)$ holds, binary search finds the least true index in $O(\log K)$ evaluations and $O(1)$ auxiliary space. This accelerates location but does not replace the monotonicity certificate.

\subsection{Cumulative construction}
Given shell counts, compute $s_n=p_n-u_n$ and update $C_n=C_{n-1}+s_n$. This takes $O(N)$ arithmetic operations and constant extra storage if only the running total and first crossing are retained. The same pass checks negativity of incoming shells or a claimed uniform deficit.

\section{Examples}
\begin{example}[Block-sampled transition]
Let $b=4$ and
\[
f(0)=7,\quad f(4)=5,\quad f(8)=2,\quad f(12)=0,\quad f(16)=-3.
\]
The unique first sampled threshold is $k_\ast=3$, and the endpoint window is $[8,12]$. Every later sampled value is negative. The linear bound gives $f(16)\le7-4=3$, with slack because several decrements exceed one.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Shell deficits]
For
\[
s_0=6,\quad s_1=-1,\quad s_2=-2,\quad s_3=-1,\quad s_4=-3,
\]
the cumulative sequence is
\[
C_0=6,\quad C_1=5,\quad C_2=3,\quad C_3=2,\quad C_4=-1.
\]
Every incoming shell after the initial shell is negative, so cumulative imbalance strictly decreases. The first nonpositive cutoff is $4$. A uniform deficit $d=1$ gives $C_4\le C_0-4=2$; the actual value is lower because two shell deficits exceed one.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Sharpness]
For a positive integer $a$, set $f(kb)=a-k$ for $0\le k\le a$. Every sampled step decreases by exactly one and the first nonpositive sample is $f(ab)=0$. Therefore the bound of $a$ blocks from initial imbalance $a$ cannot be improved without stronger assumptions.
\end{example}

\section{Interpretation and limitations}
To interpret $p_n$ and $u_n$ as formula counts, one must specify a finite alphabet and grammar, a size function, a deductive calculus, a finite proof-search bound, the shell or cumulative convention, and the meaning of ``unresolved.'' Unresolved by bounded search must not be silently identified with unprovable in principle.

Without these choices, no canonical numerical sequence exists. Synthetic examples illustrate universal implications but are not empirical evidence for a logical phenomenon. Computable recodings may redistribute formulas among length shells and shift apparent thresholds. Robust comparisons require additive or multiplicative distortion bounds for admissible translations.

Incompleteness does not itself provide negative shell drift. Existence of unprovable statements does not imply an unresolved majority in each shell, cumulative monotonicity, a sharp threshold, or a power-law distribution. Those are quantitative claims needing separate hypotheses.

Endpoint descent also permits arbitrary behavior inside blocks. Pointwise negativity after the window would require extra control, such as within-block monotonicity or bounded positive excursions. The width-$b$ window is optimal from endpoint information alone: intermediate values can place the first internal crossing anywhere in the block.

\section{Applications and discussion}
The same structure applies to any two populations accumulated by layers. A shell imbalance may represent arrivals minus completions in a queue, new cases minus recoveries, births minus deaths, or the vote margin contributed by a batch of precincts. Local contributions determine cumulative motion; block drift yields a unique sampled threshold; and integrality supplies a rate without a separately stated decrement.

The argument separates three levels of information. The shell update is exact accounting. Drift is a substantive assumption about the data. Minimality converts eventual nonpositivity into a canonical first crossing. This separation makes clear which conclusions are identities and which depend on model-specific hypotheses.

Strictness has a precise role. With weak descent, later samples remain nonpositive but need not become strictly negative after a zero. With only average negative drift, sampled values may rebound, and a permanent crossing needs a window formulation with bounded excursions.

\section{Future directions}
Natural extensions include variable-length blocks with quantitative negative drift; transition-width bounds under average drift and bounded positive excursions; concrete shell counts from a finite grammar and bounded proof checker; recoding comparisons under additive or multiplicative length distortion; and probabilistic shell deficits with high-probability crossing windows.

A probabilistic formulation must distinguish a deterministic first crossing of one realization from a statistical sharp threshold over an ensemble. Any proposed power law should state finite-range errors and coding assumptions. Connections to metric dimensions require a separately defined metric space of proofs or proof trees together with an invariance theorem relating the metric to the encoding.

\section{Conclusion}
A strictly decreasing integer-valued signal observed at finite block endpoints has a rigid transition structure. Final nonpositivity guarantees a unique first nonpositive sample, strict descent makes all later samples negative, and positive initialization localizes the change between consecutive endpoints. Integrality forces at least one unit of decay per block and bounds the crossing by the initial imbalance. For shell-generated signals, exact update identities turn local deficits into cumulative descent and linear decay. The theory is finite, reusable, and explicit about what endpoint data can and cannot establish.

\end{document}

